Wrap `db.query` in Promise, then `await` it.

```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id], (err, rows) => {
      if (err) reject(err);
      else resolve(rows);
    });
  });

  if (!rows.length) throw new Error('Not found');
  return rows[0];
}
```

**Call it:**
```javascript
// async context required
try {
  const user = await getUser(42);
} catch (err) {
  console.error(err);
}
```

**Why this shape:**
- `db.query` no return Promise natively → manual wrap needed
- Reject on `err`, resolve on `rows` → converts callback contract to Promise
- Caller use try/catch instead of `if (err)` check

**If db support Promise natively** (e.g. `mysql2/promise`), skip wrapper:
```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  const [rows] = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);
  if (!rows.length) throw new Error('Not found');
  return rows[0];
}
```
Check driver docs first. Cleaner if available.
